---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import { APPS } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/apps';
const title = 'Shopify cart recovery app directory: workflows and selection questions';
const desc = 'Explore 21 current and historical app identities, distinguish cart from checkout recovery, and plan sender ownership, migration and contribution checks.';
const legacy = new Set(['yotpo-email-sms', 'smsbump']);
const destinations = (slug: string) => slug === 'sequenzy' ? '/compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo' : `/alternatives/${slug}-alternative`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <h1 class="hero-title">Shopify cart recovery app directory</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl text-slate-700">Choose the event and the sender before choosing the app. These 21 entries preserve current and historical product identities; directory order is not a ranking. Start with the workflow distinctions below, then use each candidate’s question to prepare a focused demonstration.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify card mt-10 max-w-4xl p-8">
      <h2>Cart activity and checkout abandonment need different entry rules</h2>
      <p>Shopify distinguishes a cart reminder for someone who has not started checkout from an abandoned checkout automation. Its current documentation places marketing automations in Shopify Messaging, the product relevant to the historical Shopify Email entry below. The abandoned checkout automation defaults to email marketing subscribers. Start here when Shopify should own the reminder; document recipient eligibility and pause the overlapping sender before switching. See <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify’s automation definitions</a> and <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">checkout automation migration</a>.</p>
      <h2>Klaviyo: choose the event before building the flow</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo’s default Shopify abandoned-cart flow uses Checkout Started. Its separate Added to Cart flow reaches identifiable shoppers earlier in the journey. That distinction matters when the missing audience never reaches checkout: changing a delay will not change the entry event. Keep the earlier flow from continuing after checkout or purchase, and rehearse a shopper moving between both flows. <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/35510464880027">Klaviyo’s Added to Cart instructions</a> describe the tracking and filter setup; its <a class="link" href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Shopify data reference</a> explains checkout events and purchase exclusion.</p>
      <h2>Omnisend: coordinate the stages within the workflow</h2>
      <p>Omnisend documents separate cart and checkout automations with a handoff when a shopper starts checkout. Its broader abandonment guidance describes purchase exits and manual priority rules for other abandonment workflows. This is useful when a team wants stage-specific messages in one system, but the native handoff does not establish suppression across a separate installed sender. Inspect both queues during a controlled purchase. Read <a class="link" href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/6659889-abandoned-cart-and-abandoned-checkout-automations">cart and checkout automation guidance</a> and <a class="link" href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/4228254-manage-multiple-abandonment-automations">multiple abandonment priorities</a>.</p>
      <h2>Yotpo Email &amp; SMS and SMSBump: migration history</h2>
      <p>Yotpo announced full deprecation of Email and SMS for December 31, 2025; its SMS service now displays a decommission notice. Keep these two directory identities for migration research rather than a new recovery shortlist. Export permission history and suppression records before mapping a replacement; do not assume another Yotpo product replaces the retired sender. Sources: <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">Yotpo migration announcement</a> and <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">SMS decommission notice</a>.</p>
      <h2>Define the handoff for the remaining candidates</h2>
      <p>The directory questions distinguish sequence cancellation, subscriber capture, SMS permission, managed services, CRM ownership, delivery events and POS identity. They are evaluation tasks, not assertions of demonstrated supplier features. For capture, require the signup source and offer to arrive at the sender. For SMS, rehearse an opt-out while a reminder is queued. For managed work, put creative, setup, monitoring and exports in the service scope. For POS and online identity, decide which record owns eligibility before using a loyalty benefit.</p>
    </article>
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {APPS.map((app) => (
        <a class="card recovery-card" href={destinations(app.slug)}>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-800">{legacy.has(app.slug) ? 'Historical migration entry' : 'Evaluation question'}</span>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-black">{app.name}</h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">{legacy.has(app.slug) ? 'Retired Email/SMS identity: plan subscriber, permission and suppression migration to a replacement sender.' : app.shopifyFit}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl p-8">
      <h2>Turn a shortlist into a reproducible demonstration</h2>
      <p>Give each finalist the same basket with multiple variants and a written expected outcome. Rehearse purchase in another session, checkout after a cart reminder begins, an unavailable variant, and a permission withdrawal. Save event timestamps, queued-message state and the rendered destination link. These are proposed exercises; this directory reports no completed merchant trial or recovery lift.</p>
      <p>When a line becomes unavailable, decide whether to suppress the reminder or explain the remaining basket. Do not silently substitute a product. A repeat buyer’s offer eligibility should follow the store’s approved benefit policy rather than a universal order-count threshold. Name the operator who can pause the flow when event delivery fails.</p>
      <h2>Compare costs against the same operating plan</h2>
      <p>Request a quote using the same eligible contacts, message volumes, regions and service scope. Include sender fees, required integrations, migration work and operator time. For recovered orders, deduct discounts, goods, fulfillment, payment fees and expected returns once each. Use an experiment with a suitable observation window to estimate additional contribution; attributed sales alone cannot establish it. No fixed price or recovery percentage determines when a store must migrate.</p>
      <h2>Continue by the decision you need to make</h2>
      <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">abandoned-cart workflow guide</a> to write the operating contract, <a class="link" href="/guides">decision guides</a> for timing and incentives, and <a class="link" href="/for">store-type guides</a> for catalog constraints. Linked comparison and alternative articles are further reading; a directory link does not certify their historical claims. Keep the supplier documentation and your dated demonstration record alongside the purchasing decision.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
